-- EXPECT-ERROR: division by zero literal in `cap no_panic` module
-- Refinement types, `cap no_panic` NAME SHADOWING (2026-07-29). This program
-- passed `--check` with exit 0 and then panicked at run time with "division by
-- zero" — the failure `cap no_panic` exists to prevent.
--
-- Every channel the division checker reads keys on a bare variable NAME: the
-- path condition is `(d == 0, negated)`, a refined parameter is `("d", …)`, a
-- `let` value is `("d", rhs)`. None of them survives that name being rebound,
-- but the walk retired none of them at a binding construct, so the fact
-- `not (d == 0)` — which is about the PARAMETER — was read as though it were
-- about the inner `let d = 0`.
--
-- The same omission was reachable through a lambda parameter
-- (`ap(fn d -> 10 / d)`), a match binder (`Some(d) -> 10 / d`), and the `then`
-- side of the guard; all four are pinned in `test_refinecheck.ml`'s
-- `divsafety-shadowing` group. This one is in the corpus because the group
-- calls `Division_safety.check_module` directly, whereas the sweep runs the
-- real `--check` driver end to end.
--
-- There is no accept twin: the point is not that some shape is discharged but
-- that a rebound name discharges NOTHING. `accept/t121`'s `guarded` function
-- is the nearest thing — the same `if` guard, with the name NOT rebound, which
-- must keep exiting 0 so the retirement is not simply "distrust every guard".
mod ShadowedGuard do
  cap no_panic

  fn f(d : Int) : Int do
    if d == 0 do
      0
    else
      let d = 0
      10 / d
    end
  end

  fn main() do
    println(int_to_string(f(5)))
  end
end
